WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:02.929
("Für Elise")

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.730
- If your school had a piano chances are,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.277
you've heard this piece, at least once.

00:00:10.277 --> 00:00:12.777
("Für Elise")

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.190
Beethoven's "Für Elise" has become the

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:19.370
go-to classical piano
piece for young students.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.800
- Do you have a favorite piece

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.520
or song to play on the piano?

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.497
- Yes. "Für Elise."

00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:28.400
- Oh, that's your favorite?

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.660
That's your favorite one?

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.350
It's also been programmed
into music boxes,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.000
played on wine glasses,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.810
and used for garbage trucks in Taiwan.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.667
It's made appearances in
movies, such as "Harry Potter",

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:43.520
"Django Unchained", and a
"Charlie Brown Christmas".

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.630
- It's one of the few
classical piano pieces

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.000
that most people would recognize by name.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.620
Yet when played at serious piano recitals,

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.300
it's often met with giggles and confusion,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.470
according to pianist, Igor Levit.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.610
- It's one of Beethoven's

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.660
most recognizable melodies.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.180
But the first person who published it

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.930
wrote that it was graceful,
but not exactly important.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.810
- The piece was, of course,
written for someone named Elise,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.980
but who was Elise?

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.020
- So why is "Für Elise" so
famous and, more importantly,

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.338
does it deserve its
popularity or is it overrated?

00:01:16.338 --> 00:01:18.838
("Für Elise")

00:01:22.783 --> 00:01:23.630
- Okay, well, here's the deal?

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.510
Here's the deal.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.390
I think I'm actually in favor of the tune.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.590
I really like the song.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:29.713
- Oh no!

00:01:29.713 --> 00:01:31.780
- I was like in seventh grade, I think.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.810
Nas came out with a song called "I Can"

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.070
and it was like a little sample.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:36.903
You know what I'm saying?

00:01:36.903 --> 00:01:37.840
I was just like, "Oh, this is dope.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.150
This is the old Beethoven
tune? I can learn this."

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.582
♪ I know I can ♪

00:01:42.582 --> 00:01:45.030
♪ Be what I wanna be ♪

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.950
So I'm a fan of a song, now. I'm sorry.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:47.990
I know you're.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.220
- No, no, no.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:53.500
I am being a little facetious
about how much I dislike it.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.850
But, that being said,

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.023
I do not love it.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.573
- Before we get into whether or not

00:01:59.573 --> 00:02:01.380
"Für Elise" is overrated,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.020
let's dive into the history

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.360
and mystery surrounding
Beethoven's most famous piece.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.480
- Despite "Für Elise's" popularity today,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.060
it wasn't published until 1867,

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.610
40 years after Beethoven's death.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.620
The musicologist, Ludwig Nohl,
found it with the possessions

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.830
of one of Beethoven's
friends, Therese Malfatti.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.970
But Nohl didn't think
much about the piece.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.127
As mentioned earlier, he wrote that it was

00:02:24.127 --> 00:02:26.290
"Not exactly important."

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.900
And it's possible that
Beethoven would have agreed.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.870
In 1822, he considered
publishing it in a set of

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.100
easy piano pieces, but he decided not to.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.110
- Even though the publisher

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.090
didn't think the piece was important,

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.120
the greater public disagreed.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.350
The piece quickly grew in popularity.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.830
It was published again
several times in 1870.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:50.030
In 1900, just 33 years
after its first publication,

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.620
Tonger published "Für
Elise" in a collection

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.070
of the most popular classical
and romantic piano pieces.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.560
Development in the recording
technology of the early 1900s

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.370
allowed the piece to be
heard by mainstream audiences

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.960
and even played in movies.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.430
In the 1940s, at least three
movies included "Für Elise"

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.590
in their soundtracks.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.990
In 1965, "Für Elise" aired
on A Charlie Brown Christmas.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.687
And so, 100 years after his discovery,

00:03:15.687 --> 00:03:19.550
"Für Elise's" rise into
popular culture was cemented.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.840
- So how did "Für Elise" become so famous?

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.870
Part of the popularity probably comes from

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.610
the stardom of Beethoven.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.840
He's influenced generations
of composers and artists

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.870
throughout the 1800s and 1900s

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.170
even still today, of course.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.010
And one can get a sense
of his legendary status

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:40.133
as symbolized in this
Zeus-like sculpture from 1902.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.030
- The mystery surrounding the title

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.457
also adds to the appeal of the piece.

00:03:45.457 --> 00:03:48.710
"Für Elise" translates to "For Elise".

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.220
So who is Elise?

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:53.690
Scholars have been
fascinated by this question

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.080
and they've proposed
many different theories.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.830
Is it Therese Malfatti?

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.520
She had possession of
the piece at the time

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.720
of its discovery.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.230
In addition, Beethoven was
once wildly in love with her.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.530
But her name isn't Elise.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:08.990
Or, is it Elisabeth Rockel?

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.000
She was a close friend of Beethoven.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.430
But then, how did Therese
gain possession of the piece?

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.660
Or was it another Elise entirely,

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.910
like the child prodigy, Elise Barensfeld.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.670
She lived across the street
from Therese Malfatti.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.030
These theories have appeared
in newspapers, magazines,

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.540
and even documentaries.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.303
But the identity of
Elise remains unsolved.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.520
And while it's unclear,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.510
whether Elise was one of
Beethoven's love interests,

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.030
popular culture has picked up on the idea.

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:40.727
The comments of YouTube
recordings are full of jokes like,

00:04:40.727 --> 00:04:42.800
"Maybe if Elise hadn't rejected him,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.840
I would have been able to play this."

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.760
- At the time "Für Elise"
was discovered in the 1860s,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.360
there was an increase in demand

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.830
for relatively easy piano music.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.410
This was due to a growth
in a wealthy middle-class

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.220
and advancements in piano design

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.560
that drastically increased
piano ownership in Europe.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.010
Pianos became smaller and more affordable,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.750
so they were accessible to more people.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.890
Now let's dive into the
music of "Für Elise"

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:08.150
to see what, specifically,

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.850
may have contributed to its popularity.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.930
For one, the opening
sounds rather complicated.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.165
Yet, it's still very
approachable for piano students.

00:05:17.165 --> 00:05:19.440
("Für Elise")

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.920
- Why do you think the song
"Für Elise" is so popular?

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.340
- Because it's a little bit complicated.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:29.927
Nothing can be too fun if
it's not complicated enough.

00:05:32.594 --> 00:05:34.310
- A little bit of challenge in there.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:35.170
- Yep.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.157
- Ms. Emily, why do you decide to teach

00:05:38.157 --> 00:05:40.130
"Für Elise" to your students?

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.050
- I've been teaching piano
for about 20 years now

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.150
and I've realized that the first section

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:47.150
of "Für Elise" is
actually pretty achievable

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.210
to most intermediate students.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.930
And what's interesting is, I think

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.140
that, the tune that so
many people have heard,

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:55.870
that the kids really feel like

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.210
now I've arrived as a piano player.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.860
You know, I'm not playing those songs

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.450
that are just in the lesson books.

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.490
This is like a real
song by a real composer.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.490
- Another reason why "Für
Elise" may be so popular

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:12.410
is because its structure is
very similar to many pop songs.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.937
Pop songs often use a verse
chorus bridge structure.

00:06:15.937 --> 00:06:19.550
"Für Elise" uses a similar
structure called the Rondo form.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.960
The Rondo form typically
consists of a main theme

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.720
alternating with supporting
themes and sections.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.455
Here is Theme A of "Für Elise."

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.955
("Für Elise")

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:35.419
Theme B.

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:37.919
("Für Elise")

00:06:40.913 --> 00:06:42.304
And theme C.

00:06:42.304 --> 00:06:44.887
("Für Elise")

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:50.960
Notice how the verse chorus bridge form

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.980
and the Rondo form are nearly identical.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.850
The overall effect is that
the A theme is repeated

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.640
enough times that the
listener can recall the tune

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.960
after listening to it once.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.740
It's kind of catchy.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.210
("Für Elise")

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.550
We've mapped out "Für
Elise's" rise in fame,

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.590
but does it really deserve its popularity?

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:13.800
Let's consider what a
"Für Elise" enthusiast

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.660
and a "Für Elise" critic might
have to say about the piece.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.728
Let's first talk about the melody.

00:07:19.728 --> 00:07:22.228
("Für Elise")

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:26.668
Definitely singable, definitely

00:07:26.668 --> 00:07:28.970
lovely and memorable.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:32.483
But does it have a profound quality?

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:36.730
Not really. It's very
casual, it's sing songy.

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:38.260
I just think that it lacks

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:41.550
in the emotional depth
of the melody itself

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.104
combined with the harmony.

00:07:43.104 --> 00:07:44.940
("Für Elise")

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.430
And it doesn't hold this tension

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.670
that I think a lot of great melodies do.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.050
For example, Beethoven's second movement

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.178
from his Pathétique Sonata.

00:07:53.178 --> 00:07:56.920
("Pathétique Sonata")

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:58.670
There's so much tension there.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:03.670
It's very delicate
because each chord change

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:06.150
makes you hold your breath.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.316
And I feel

00:08:07.316 --> 00:08:09.780
("Für Elise")

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.110
I feel like I can breathe very
easily during "Für Elise."

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:14.680
I do think it's catchier

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.730
than many of his other
bagatelles but it's hard to say

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:22.490
that catchiness can be a
merit onto itself because

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:25.920
think of nursery rhymes,
for example, they're great.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.410
Of course they're
memorable. They're catchy.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:33.000
But is Humpty Dumpty the
most profound thing ever?

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.150
- Popular doesn't mean it's good, but

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:39.170
I think it is popular
because there are some merits

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:41.620
in these things like
his repetition, I think.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:46.620
So, yes, it is miniature
and seems kind of like

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:48.620
there's no finality to it.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.784
But, I think that's a feature
and not a bug as they say,

00:08:51.784 --> 00:08:52.810
of this piece.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.300
It's also great because
it repeats forever.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.670
It's like background
music for a video game,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.000
something, you can almost,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.260
you would maybe get tired of it.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:00.970
But I think most people,

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.299
they could hear that on
repeat for a long time

00:09:03.299 --> 00:09:04.620
and it kind of just
settles in that background

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.860
and it gives us sort of comfort to it.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.530
- You're almost changing
my mind about "Für Elise."

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.010
But I would add one thing.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:17.010
I think the melody is not
quite as melancholic nor tragic

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:20.800
nor as longing as people
give it credit for.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.080
- I think one of the most
beautiful things actually is

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.370
in the main theme when
he goes to C major right?

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:26.521
You...

00:09:26.521 --> 00:09:28.750
("Für Elise")

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.250
Sorry, I'm skipping a lot of notes.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:31.403
And then.

00:09:31.403 --> 00:09:33.903
("Für Elise")

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:36.043
You're back.

00:09:36.043 --> 00:09:40.630
He always tries to go
to C major, brighter.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:43.461
We know when Beethoven
writes C major, it's like

00:09:43.461 --> 00:09:47.530
(intense C Major music)

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:50.590
Right, very heroic, bright kind of thing.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.640
And he tries to go there but,

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.820
he just sequences back down into A minor.

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:57.360
I think there's something
beautiful about that.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.420
- The last question that I
really wanted to ask you is that

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.800
if you had a student beginner
to intermediate level that

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:06.080
was looking to learn a new piece,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.397
would you and have you ever assigned

00:10:08.397 --> 00:10:12.050
"Für Elise" before it was requested?

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:15.640
- I have not assigned "Für
Elise" before it was requested.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.080
I could definitely see
myself assigning it,

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.930
depending on the student, but
no, I have not assigned it.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:23.293
Ever.

00:10:23.293 --> 00:10:24.920
- Josef just cut that
we that's all we need.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.390
We can clip that.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:29.340
That's all that matters and we can use it.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.400
Just kidding.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.530
- Since "Für Elise" isn't as important

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.580
as some of Beethoven's other works,

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.030
it seems strange that
it became so popular.

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.710
But, this is a common trend in music.

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:43.230
For example, Johann Pachelbel
helped develop the "Fugue"

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.040
and the "Chorale Prelude"
during the Baroque period.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.520
Yet, several hundred
years after his death,

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.270
Pachelbel's Canon became
his most popular work.

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:54.490
The piece itself is not
that musically complex.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:57.290
It includes a cello line that
repeats the same eight notes

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.081
for five minutes straight.

00:10:59.081 --> 00:11:02.248
("Pachelbel's Canon")

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:12.740
- Today. You can hear
"Für Elise" in almost

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.840
every part of the globe, from
ice cream trucks to TV shows.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:18.170
It's even gone commercial

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.648
and featured in many advertisements.

00:11:20.648 --> 00:11:21.481
("Für Elise")

00:11:21.481 --> 00:11:22.314
♪ And not give any ♪

00:11:22.314 --> 00:11:23.723
♪ To my dumb brother ♪

00:11:23.723 --> 00:11:25.803
♪ Can't offer mine oh mine ♪

00:11:25.803 --> 00:11:28.670
- "Für Elise" continues to
inspire musicians today.

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:31.960
The legend Ennio Morricone
remixed "Für Elise" into

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:35.620
a Western score that opens
the film Inglorious Bastards.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:38.080
Listen for how the music
communicates tension

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:41.020
by contrasting "Für
Elise's" simple melody line

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.154
with a dramatic guitar part.

00:11:43.154 --> 00:11:46.154
("Für Elise" remix)

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:49.830
You can see how today's artists

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.620
use the familiarity of "Für Elise"

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:54.620
to communicate complex emotions and ideas.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:57.140
This leads us to our friend, Andre Sims,

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.540
who composed his own
rendition of "Für Elise"

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.340
and mixed it with his jazzy style

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:03.750
to create something
completely new and surprising.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:06.490
- Can you explain why
you chose "Für Elise"

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.420
to make an arrangement out of it,

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.660
what attracted you to it,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.070
and what the process was like?

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:14.730
- As a teacher, I was like, okay, well,

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:15.770
I hear this song so much.

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.340
Even when I heard other teachers

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.077
teaching from other rooms, for example,

00:12:19.077 --> 00:12:20.700
"Für Elise" was playing.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.780
It was just becoming
externally exhausting to hear.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:27.780
I did this in a live show at a jazz show

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.360
and it was on the wing.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:30.320
We didn't practice it.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:31.540
After the show, it was like,

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:33.300
everybody's giving me a standing ovation.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:34.850
All the jazz songs we did that night,

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:36.020
it was like, great, great.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:38.650
But that one song people
was asking me like over

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:40.047
and over again after the show,

00:12:40.047 --> 00:12:41.590
"Hey, where could we hear that at?

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:43.057
Like where this at? Is this on iTunes?

00:12:43.057 --> 00:12:45.770
I'm like, no, it's just

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:47.188
the way that I can cope with my students.

00:12:47.188 --> 00:12:49.032
(laughing)

00:12:49.032 --> 00:12:50.980
Can you guys hear this good?

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:51.820
- Yeah, I can hear it.

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:53.670
- I'm gonna play it the way I started off

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:54.503
like I did in the show.

00:12:54.503 --> 00:12:55.613
So it was like.

00:12:55.613 --> 00:12:58.113
("Für Elise")

00:13:05.957 --> 00:13:08.957
("Für Elise" remix)

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:19.600
- So by now you hopefully
have a better idea of

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:22.040
why "Für Elise" has become
one of the most famous

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:24.250
classical pieces in the world.

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:26.370
When it comes to whether
or not it's overrated,

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:28.370
well, that depends on who you ask.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.650
For some, it's way too overplayed.

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:33.970
But to others, it's their
favorite piece to perform.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:37.070
What's more important is
that "Für Elise" has somehow

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:40.592
now become a permanent
part of our culture.

00:14:40.592 --> 00:14:43.592
("Für Elise" remix)

00:14:47.204 --> 00:14:48.037
- Oh. Awesome.

00:14:48.037 --> 00:14:48.930
Awesome. Awesome.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.034
Amazing, amazing, amazing.

00:14:51.034 --> 00:14:52.130
(clapping)

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.690
- Have you ever wondered what
makes words, well, words?

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:58.400
Otherwords, PBS's newest show

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.530
on language and linguistics,
has you covered.

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:02.860
- From determining the origins of language

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:05.944
to whether or not computers
can actually talk,

00:15:05.944 --> 00:15:08.790
otherwords dives into
languages around the world

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.300
